[SPEAKER_00] You now understand what the app does.

831
01:03:51,680 --> 01:03:54,960
[SPEAKER_00] And they've done that by asking a question and giving you multiple choice answers.

832
01:03:55,360 --> 01:04:02,720
[SPEAKER_00] But beyond that, you can use, I like to call them interludes, which are kind of the screens that break up questions to communicate specific information.

833
01:04:03,280 --> 01:04:17,120
[SPEAKER_00] I think there are three types of content you could put on an interlude, like evidence, things that prove you do what you say you do, whether that be charts, graphs, social proof, testimonials, the sorts content that focuses on reassuring the user.

834
01:04:17,600 --> 01:04:19,440
[SPEAKER_00] There may be known anxieties that they have.

835
01:04:19,920 --> 01:04:23,120
[SPEAKER_00] I think there's a great example from Headway that's like, do you often worry?

836
01:04:23,280 --> 01:04:25,040
[SPEAKER_00] Or something like this, like, how often do you worry?

837
01:04:25,360 --> 01:04:29,600
[SPEAKER_00] And then you click a lot and then it tells you, hey, don't worry, a lot of people worry, stuff like this.

838
01:04:29,920 --> 01:04:39,280
[SPEAKER_00] That's an example of reassuring the user, but the idea is you want to figure out prior to building what those are and then on certain screens call them out specifically.

839
01:04:40,000 --> 01:04:41,760
[SPEAKER_00] A great example is time to value.

840
01:04:42,240 --> 01:04:45,760
[SPEAKER_00] I don't know how long it's going to take for me to get my abs summers next week.

841
01:04:46,160 --> 01:04:46,640
[SPEAKER_00] Can I do it?

842
01:04:47,120 --> 01:04:48,880
[SPEAKER_00] This app told me I can, whoohoo.

843
01:04:49,280 --> 01:04:51,200
[SPEAKER_00] So the charts we see, but that's one.

844
01:04:51,440 --> 01:04:53,360
[SPEAKER_00] And the final one is I would say functionality.

845
01:04:54,080 --> 01:05:01,360
[SPEAKER_00] Don't not talk about your product, you want them to get to the end of onboarding and have a pretty good understanding of what you're going to do and how.

846
01:05:02,000 --> 01:05:03,040
[SPEAKER_00] So that's something.

847
01:05:03,360 --> 01:05:06,000
[SPEAKER_00] And doing those things ultimately adds value.

848
01:05:06,720 --> 01:05:08,400
[SPEAKER_00] So that's kind of my approach.

849
01:05:08,880 --> 01:05:18,320
[SPEAKER_00] I guess if I had to wrap it up in a nice way, I would say that a good web onboarding journey tells a story, a great web onboarding journey echoes their story back to them.

850
01:05:18,800 --> 01:05:20,000
[SPEAKER_00] Next up is social proof.

851
01:05:20,320 --> 01:05:26,320
[SPEAKER_02] How do you incorporate social proof effectively and then how effective is it and should people be using social proof?

852
01:05:26,720 --> 01:05:29,520
[SPEAKER_00] I often get asked what type of social proof we should use.

853
01:05:30,160 --> 01:06:13,280
[SPEAKER_00] And I think it really comes down to what questions back to the previous comment the users have in their head like are they thinking do i believe this works are they thinking is this safe are they thinking if it's like a very health oriented product you're asking for medical information is this a trusted product who's validated this who approves them you know whatever these kind of questions are but the idea is what objections do people have once you identify those then you can narrow down what social proof you should be displaying on your paywall not your paywall your web onboarding journey or your paywall i think a great example is and you've probably seen it all the time when people have like 80 million 220 million, 30 million users use this product.

854
01:06:13,760 --> 01:06:19,840
[SPEAKER_00] It's like, that's great, but probably if 20, 50, 80 million people use the product, I already know what the product is.

855
01:06:20,400 --> 01:06:27,920
[SPEAKER_00] So smaller apps who are then like, join thousands of people using my app, it's like, it doesn't really have the same impact.

856
01:06:28,240 --> 01:06:37,280
[SPEAKER_00] You're better off saying, join others who have X, Y, Z like you, and then having a testimonial of someone just like you who solved that problem.

857
01:06:37,760 --> 01:06:42,800
[SPEAKER_00] It's less focused on kind of volume and quantity, more focused on kind of qualitative efforts.

858
01:06:43,280 --> 01:06:46,240
[SPEAKER_02] And lastly, talk to me about personalization.

859
01:06:46,640 --> 01:06:46,960
[SPEAKER_02] How when?

860
01:06:46,960 --> 01:06:50,720
[SPEAKER_02] How, when, why should you think about personalizing?

861
01:06:51,040 --> 01:07:02,320
[SPEAKER_02] And this does get to a point where it can get very complex to personalize a web journey where you've got this like infinite branches of a tree, but I know it can be powerful.

862
01:07:02,480 --> 01:07:04,320
[SPEAKER_02] So tell me about how you think about personalization.

863
01:07:04,640 --> 01:07:06,880
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah, I think personalization is super key.

864
01:07:07,520 --> 01:07:12,960
[SPEAKER_00] I think the reason it's so important is because it's how you echo their story back to them.

865
01:07:13,360 --> 01:07:20,160
[SPEAKER_00] If I just have a really nice flow and ask some great questions, I'm saying stuff and I'm doing stuff, but I don't know how relevant it is to you.

866
01:07:20,800 --> 01:07:24,240
[SPEAKER_00] What I want to do is make you believe that this product was designed for you.

867
01:07:24,640 --> 01:07:27,840
[SPEAKER_00] Like someone sat down and thought about David and then built the app.

868
01:07:28,480 --> 01:07:35,520
[SPEAKER_00] And the way to do that is to ask questions, use the answers in the next questions and kind of take them on that journey.

869
01:07:36,320 --> 01:07:52,000
[SPEAKER_00] What I would say though is that you don't need to over invest in this at the beginning because oftentimes the diminishing returns are very real and you think that adding way more personalization is going to dramatically increase your conversion rate and it doesn't because people don't care that much.

870
01:07:52,640 --> 01:07:56,880
[SPEAKER_00] So I think the question is to figure out what do people actually care about?

871
01:07:57,520 --> 01:08:01,520
[SPEAKER_00] For example, if we take an example of an app that helps you get to sleep, right?

872
01:08:01,760 --> 01:08:06,240
[SPEAKER_00] If you're helping me get to sleep and you're asking me what time I sleep and all this stuff, great.

873
01:08:06,560 --> 01:08:09,920
[SPEAKER_00] You're going to personalize it by reminding me when I can go to sleep.

874
01:08:10,320 --> 01:08:10,480
[SPEAKER_00] Great.

875
01:08:10,640 --> 01:08:11,040
[SPEAKER_00] This is cool.

876
01:08:11,680 --> 01:08:14,560
[SPEAKER_00] But that's not really moving the needle for me emotionally, right?

877
01:08:14,800 --> 01:08:15,360
[SPEAKER_00] It's not doing much.

878
01:08:15,920 --> 01:08:20,640
[SPEAKER_00] It's very functional and I understand, but it's not probably the best example of personalization.

879
01:08:21,200 --> 01:08:35,840
[SPEAKER_00] Whereas if we take an app that is to do with improving your focus at work and you're asking me what my job is and then you're telling me that, you know, CEOs XYZ developers XYZ, you know, train drivers ABC.

880
01:08:36,160 --> 01:08:38,080
[SPEAKER_00] Now this is like, ah, okay, this makes sense.

881
01:08:38,560 --> 01:08:40,800
[SPEAKER_00] because it's specifically for me in my context.

882
01:08:41,520 --> 01:08:51,200
[SPEAKER_00] So I think focusing personalization on context as opposed to just focusing personalization on questions, answers is a nice way to wrap it up.

883
01:08:51,520 --> 01:08:53,120
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah, that's some fantastic advice.

